>
  <h1>Terms</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Bubo is a macOS app made by Weeknd Labs. These terms cover using it and
    buying a Pro key. Plain language, because you should be able to read them.
  </p>

  <h2>The two plans</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Personal</strong> is free. No account, no time limit, no nagging. It
    is the same application as Pro.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pro</strong> is a one-off payment of $29. It gives you a license key
    valid on up to three Macs that you own or use, and updates for as long as
    this major version is current. It is not a subscription and it does not
    renew.
  </p>

  <h2>Your key</h2>
  <p>
    The key is issued to your account and is yours to use on your own machines.
    Do not publish it or sell it on. You can move a key between your own Macs by
    freeing a slot.
  </p>
  <p>
    A key stops being valid if the purchase behind it is refunded or charged
    back. Bubo then goes back to Personal, which keeps working.
  </p>

  <h2>Buying</h2>
  <p>
    Payments are taken by Paddle, who are the merchant of record. Your contract
    for the purchase is with them, their terms apply to the transaction, and the
    receipt comes from them. The price you see at checkout includes whatever tax
    your country charges. Refunds are covered on the
    <a href="/refund">refunds page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not promise</h2>
  <p>
    Bubo is provided as it is. We work on it and we fix what we can, but we
    cannot promise it is free of faults or that it suits a particular purpose.
    Nothing here takes away rights you have under the consumer law where you
    live.
  </p>

  <h2>Changing these terms</h2>
  <p>
    If these terms change, the version in force for your purchase is the one on
    this page on the day you bought. The date below says when it last moved.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting in touch</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="mailto:support@weekndlabs.com">support@weekndlabs.com</a>. A person
    reads it.
  </p>
</Legal>
